# Mutual Non-Disclosure Agreement — N-5 Term and survival

> Module Nda_survival

This module answers one question: until when do the N-3 obligations of
confidence bind? Nothing else in NDA-MUT is computable, and that is the
correct outcome rather than a gap — N-1 to N-4 and N-6 to N-9 are qualitative
throughout and belong to the vector store.

N-5.1 fixes the end of the Agreement and N-5.2 measures the survival period
**from that end**, so the two compose: early termination on 30 days' notice
moves the survival end date *earlier*, never later. N-5.3 and N-5.4 are
sibling exceptions to N-5.2, each displacing the 3-year arithmetic for the
class of information it covers.

`survival_end_date` is an `optional of date`. `Absent` means **no expiry date
is produced**, which is the only honest answer where N-5.3 governs: the
terminus of a trade-secret obligation is a future legal characterisation, not
a date. The companion output `obligations_subsist` answers, as at an
`assessment_date`, whether the obligations are still on foot — that is where
the judgement input `information_remains_trade_secret` does its work.

## Prologue — declarations

`information_is_trade_secret` and `information_remains_trade_secret` are the
two **judgement inputs** of N-5.3 (see `triage/decisions.yaml`). Whether
information "constitutes a trade secret" is a legal characterisation the
Agreement does not define and which must be re-made over time; this module
therefore never decides it, and never asserts an expiry date for information
so characterised.

By deliberate contrast, `is_personal_data` and `date_ceased_to_hold` are
**ordinary inputs**: the class of the information is recorded in the
processing records and continued holding is an inventory fact. N-5.4 is RULE,
not HYBRID, and the contrast with N-5.3 is load-bearing.

`date_ceased_to_hold` is `Absent` while the Recipient still holds the personal
data, and `Present content d` where the data inventory records that it ceased
to hold it on `d`. `termination_notice_date` is `Absent` where no party has
served notice under N-5.1.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope SurvivalEnd:
  input effective_date content date
  input termination_notice_date content optional of date
  input is_personal_data content boolean
  input date_ceased_to_hold content optional of date
  input information_is_trade_secret content boolean
  input information_remains_trade_secret content boolean
  input assessment_date content date
  output agreement_end_date content date
  output survival_end_date content optional of date
  output obligations_subsist content boolean
```

| NO-CLAUSE: the rounding mode is a compiler directive, not a rule. Catala
| date addition raises on an ambiguous result, so the scope must declare one.
| `date round up` is chosen because N-5.1 and N-5.2 confer periods of
| protection — the Agreement "continues for a period of 2 years" and the
| obligations "continue for a period of 3 years" — so where a calendar month
| has no corresponding day (an Effective Date of 29 February) the period is
| taken to the next existing day rather than ending a day early. Rounding
| down would curtail an obligation of confidence by a calendar artefact.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  date round up
```

## N-5 Term and survival

| NDA-MUT N-5.1 (005-mutual-nda.md:71)
|
| This Agreement takes effect on the Effective Date and continues for a
| period of 2 years, unless terminated earlier by either party on 30 days'
| written notice.

The clause states a term and one exception to it. The exception is encoded as
a Catala exception rather than folded into a conditional, because "unless
terminated earlier" is exactly that.

Notice does not automatically end the Agreement: it ends it only where the
30 days expire **before** the second anniversary, which is what "terminated
earlier" requires. Notice served within the last 30 days of the term
therefore leaves the ordinary expiry undisturbed.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_1_term definition agreement_end_date
    equals effective_date + 2 year

  label n5_1_notice exception n5_1_term definition agreement_end_date
    under condition
      (match termination_notice_date with pattern
       -- Absent: false
       -- Present content notice:
            notice + 30 day < effective_date + 2 year)
    consequence equals
      (match termination_notice_date with pattern
       -- Absent: effective_date + 2 year
       -- Present content notice: notice + 30 day)
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.2 (005-mutual-nda.md:75)
|
| The obligations of confidence in N-3 survive expiry or termination of this
| Agreement and continue for a period of 3 years from the date of expiry or
| termination.

The 3 years run from `agreement_end_date`, which is the date of expiry *or*
termination as N-5.1 determines it. This is the composition point: an early
termination under N-5.1 pulls the survival end date back with it.

The end date is the last day on which the obligations bind, so
`obligations_subsist` compares with `<=`. This is the base case of the
hierarchy; N-5.3 and N-5.4 are exceptions to it.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    equals Present content (agreement_end_date + 3 year)

  label n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    equals assessment_date <= agreement_end_date + 3 year
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.3 (005-mutual-nda.md:79)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| Confidential Information which constitutes a trade secret continue for so
| long as that information remains a trade secret.

**HYBRID.** Given the judgement that the information constitutes a trade
secret, the deterministic consequence is that the N-5.2 arithmetic is
disapplied and **no expiry date is produced**. Returning a 3-year date here
would be the module confidently asserting the one answer the clause
contradicts; returning an invented indefinite date would be worse. `Absent`
is the encoding of "this clause fixes no date".

Whether the obligations are still on foot is a separate question, and it is
the second judgement input that answers it: they subsist for so long as the
information remains a trade secret. Where it has ceased to be one, the
obligations have ended — but on a date this module cannot know, so it still
produces none.

The condition excludes information that is also personal data, because N-5.4
is a sibling exception to the same base and both would otherwise apply to the
same information. The combined case is dealt with expressly below.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_3 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and not is_personal_data
    consequence equals Absent

  label n5_3_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and not is_personal_data
    consequence equals information_remains_trade_secret
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.4 (005-mutual-nda.md:83)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| personal data continue for so long as the Recipient holds that personal
| data.

**RULE, not HYBRID.** Both elements are records rather than judgements, so
the end date is computable: it is the date the Recipient ceased to hold the
personal data. While it still holds it, no end date has yet arisen, so
`Absent` is returned for that reason and not N-5.3's.

Note that N-5.4 can shorten as well as lengthen the survival: personal data
returned or destroyed under N-6.1 one year after termination ends the
obligation then, well before the N-5.2 three years.

The condition repeats nothing from `n5_2` because `n5_2` is unconditional; it
does exclude the trade-secret case, for the reason given under N-5.3.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_4 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      is_personal_data and not information_is_trade_secret
    consequence equals date_ceased_to_hold

  label n5_4_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      is_personal_data and not information_is_trade_secret
    consequence equals
      (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
       -- Absent: true
       -- Present content ceased: assessment_date <= ceased)
```

## N-5.3 and N-5.4 applying to the same information

| NDA-MUT N-5.3 (005-mutual-nda.md:79)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| Confidential Information which constitutes a trade secret continue for so
| long as that information remains a trade secret.

| NDA-MUT N-5.4 (005-mutual-nda.md:83)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| personal data continue for so long as the Recipient holds that personal
| data.

Information can be both a trade secret and personal data, and then both
exceptions bear on it. The Agreement states no precedence between them, so
this block encodes the interaction rather than either clause alone — hence
the two quotations.

Neither clause disapplies the other: each independently continues the N-3
obligations for its own period. So the obligations subsist while **either**
period runs, and no end date is produced, because the trade-secret period
produces none and is therefore never the shorter of the two.

Writing this limb out is what keeps the two sibling exceptions mutually
exclusive. Left overlapping they would both apply and Catala would raise
`Conflict` at runtime — a defect the type checker does not catch.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_34 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and is_personal_data
    consequence equals Absent

  label n5_34_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and is_personal_data
    consequence equals
      information_remains_trade_secret
      or (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
          -- Absent: true
          -- Present content ceased: assessment_date <= ceased)
```
